  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 337 PM EDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nIt's a pleasant day across southern Indiana and north central \nKentucky as the area of lower clouds has pushed southward into \nsouthern Kentucky. Temperatures have climbed into the upper 60s/low \n70s, with 7-12 mph northeasterly winds. While some lingering clouds \ncould persist into tonight south of the Parkways, some clearing \nshould allow more places in the southern ports of the CWA to \nexperience some sunshine. \n\nFor tonight and into tomorrow, a couple of features will skirt by \nthe region before surface high pressure gets reinforced. A shortwave \ntrough and associated weak surface cold front will swing over the \nGreat lakes Region, grazing the far northern portions of the CWA. \nAnother weak wave will pass through the Tennessee Valley, with both \nfeatures exiting off to our east by Sunday evening. While the \nnorthern half of the area will contain fairly little moisture aloft, \nthe northern periphery of a moisture field that extends south to the \nGulf will provide enough moisture (1-1.5\" PW) to allow for slight \nchances of rain showers early Sunday morning into early Sunday \nafternoon near the Lake Cumberland region. For places north, expect \na dry and mostly sunny day with highs in the mid to upper 70s. Some \npatchy river valley fog is also possible tonight, especially for \nplaces that get calm winds and clear skies.\n\nWith surface high pressure being reinforced over the region \nbeginning Sunday night, another stretch of dry and comfortable \nconditions will be in store. Weaker NNE flow will prevail through \nthe early portions of the week, and with decent radiational cooling \nexpected Monday and Tuesday night, temperatures will fall into the \n40s/low 50s. Recent guidance did hint at the potential for upper 30s \nlows Tuesday morning near the Bluegrass Region, but recent \ndeterministic and ensemble guidance has trended away from this \nsolution, shifting the area of coldest temperatures off to the north \nand east. Daytime temperatures will be close to normal for this time \nof year through Tuesday, with highs in the low to mid 70s along and \nnorth of I-64 and in the mid to upper 70s for places south.\n\nThe area of surface high pressure shifts to the east by Wednesday, \nallowing southerly flow to return to the region. Afternoon highs \nWednesday and Thursday will be in the upper 70s/low 80s, increasing \ninto the low to mid 80s by Friday and Saturday. Decent radiational \ncooling will keep temperatures on the cooler end, with lows in the \nupper 40s/low 50s forecast. Saturday morning temperatures will be \nslightly warmer, with lows in the low to mid 50s expected. While \nthere is uncertainty on the arrival, our next chance for potential \nrainfall comes late Saturday into Sunday as a surge of tropical \nmoisture pushes northward towards the region.",
